Fix: build MCP on Windows -- gate EventSource by capability, not by "not Linux"

swift-sdk's manifest links the EventSource product on Apple platforms only:

    .product(name: "EventSource", package: "eventsource",
             condition: .when(platforms: [.macOS, .iOS, .tvOS, .visionOS, .watchOS, .macCatalyst]))

but HTTPClientTransport.swift gates its use with #if !os(Linux). On Windows that guard is TRUE
while the module is absent, so the MCP target fails to build:

    Sources/MCP/Base/Transports/HTTPClientTransport.swift:5:12: error: no such module 'EventSource'

EventSource cannot simply be linked on Windows instead -- it depends on swift-nio and declares only
Apple platforms -- so Windows belongs on the same no-SSE path Linux already takes. Expressing the
condition as the capability it actually is fixes Windows and stays correct for any future platform,
since canImport is false exactly when the manifest did not link the module.

Verified against swift-sdk 0.12.1 on Windows 11 / Swift 6.3.3 (x86_64-unknown-windows-msvc):
the MCP module builds, and a downstream server using StatefulHTTPServerTransport answers a real
initialize handshake over HTTP.

Applies to Sources/MCP/Base/Transports/HTTPClientTransport.swift, 5 conditional sites.
